Subject: Branch on null and nullish values with one register instruction

What this does
  Adds four register opcodes, `branch_null`, `branch_not_null`,
  `branch_nullish` and `branch_not_nullish`, each taking one register and a
  branch target. The branch fusion folds a `null` or `undefined` load, the
  loose or strict comparison against it and the conditional branch into one of
  them, and folds an `is_null` test followed by a conditional branch the same
  way. Loose comparisons keep their annex B behavior: an IsHTMLDDA object is
  nullish.

Why we need it
  `x != null` took three dispatches (load the constant, compare through the
  generic loose-equality path, branch). In Octane richards the null load alone
  is 7.7% of executed instructions, and the loose compare another 2.3% of its
  machine instructions; earleyboyer spends 3.4% of its dispatches on
  `is_null` followed by a branch.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  Yes, it adds register opcodes; 9999-bc-version-bump covers it and rebuilds
  the register builtins.


